    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/CIHR-Research-Fellow_JR-018794": {
      "title": "CIHR Research Fellow",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-30\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-term (2 years). Anticipated commencement: Late January 2027   Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences  Department/School: Melbourne School of Public Health – Population Interventions Unit  Salary: Level A: AUD $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super  Global tobacco control research across five countries / innovative SHINE health intervention modelling / strong coaching and mentoring within a growing team  Join a world-leading public health team and apply your analytical skills to shape global tobacco policy.   Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.     The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity   Excellent opportunity to forge a career path at the intersection of epidemiology, economics, data science, tobacco control and other public health interventions.   You will contribute to cutting-edge tobacco control modelling, informing health and equity impacts across multiple countries.  You will also expand your expertise through SHINE projects spanning risk factors, cost-effectiveness, and health inequality analysis.  You will join a collaborative, internationally connected team, supported to build your research profile and future funding.   What you will deliver:    Lead conceptualisation and specification of tobacco control and other prevention interventions across diverse country contexts.  Conduct literature reviews, data synthesis, and curation of external datasets to inform simulation models and intervention design.  Analyse survey and other data to generate model inputs, including smoothing estimates and forecasting epidemiological trends.  Collaborate with international GPrev-Tobacco partners and SHINE colleagues, running Python models and checking outputs for accuracy.  Lead and contribute to publications, grant applications, and stakeholder engagement that translate modelling insights into policy impact.     You may be a great fit if you:    Hold a PhD or near completion in epidemiology, economics, demography, or a closely related quantitative discipline.  Demonstrate strong analytical and problem-solving ability, with experience conducting independent and collaborative research projects.  Possess proficiency in at least one statistical or programming language, most importantly Python, but also R and STATA.  Use Microsoft Excel confidently for data management, analysis, and preparation of inputs for simulation models.  Bring experience or strong interest in tobacco control or other health risk factors, such as diet or obesity.  Value working in multidisciplinary, international teams, building relationships with diverse collaborators and contributing to shared outputs.  For further information please refer to the attached PD.   What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!   Your new team – Melbourne School of Public Health   You will join the Population Interventions Unit within the Melbourne School of Public Health, a global leader.  The team specialises in innovative modelling of prevention and its health, health inequality, and economic impacts to inform real-world policy decisions.  You will work closely with Professor Tony Blakely and colleagues in a supportive, collaborative research environment.  The Unit is deeply connected to international partners, offering rich exposure to global public health challenges.   Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.  A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria , found in the Position Description below.  Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Professor Tony Blakely  For questions related to our recruitment process email Vanessa Phan at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: 14 October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.    Position Description: PD_JR-018794 CIHR Research Fellow.docx   PD_JR-018794 CIHR Research Fellow.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Research-Strategy-Project-Officer_JR-017516": {
      "title": "Research Strategy Project Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-30\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed term (10 months)  Department: Melbourne Research and Enterprise, Chancellery  Salary: UOM 7 - $111,275 - $120,453 p.a. plus 17% super  Strategic role Advancing Research 2030 / collaborate with senior leaders and stakeholder’s / coordinate flagship impact initiatives  Step into a central strategy role and help shape research impact and translation across the University.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity:   You will coordinate strategic research impact projects that support the University’s Advancing Research 2030 ambitions.  You will gain high-level exposure through preparing briefs, reports and committee support for senior University leaders.  What you will deliver:   Prepare clear, concise research, analysis, reports, correspondence, briefings and presentations for senior University leaders.  Coordinate and administer research impact projects, including frameworks, ecosystem mapping, forums and related strategic initiatives.  Manage end-to-end delivery of the Lens of Discovery Photography Competition and the Reel Impact Prize.  Maintain and update research impact content across digital platforms, including the Research Hub and Research Gateway.  Provide high-quality secretariat support for key committees and working groups, ensuring actions and deadlines are met.  You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a degree-level qualification and bring relevant experience in project coordination or policy-focused environments.  Demonstrate strong written communication skills, producing clear, concise reports, briefings and presentations for senior audiences.  Exhibit proven project coordination capability, managing timelines, documentation and delivery across multiple concurrent activities.  Conduct thorough desktop research, synthesising complex information into evidence-based advice and options.  Build effective relationships with diverse stakeholders and collaborate across teams in large, complex organisations.  Handle sensitive information with discretion while exercising sound judgement and practical problem-solving skills.  What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team:   You will join Melbourne Research and Enterprise, a collaborative team driving the University’s research strategy and impact.  The portfolio brings together research strategy, management and enterprise functions to support world-class, socially impactful research.  You will work with colleagues across Chancellery and Faculties, gaining broad networks and insight into institutional decision-making.  Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Matthew Holmes at m.holmes@unimelb.edu.au    For questions related to our recruitment process email Alessandro Storani at alessandro.storani@unimelb.edu.au    For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: ​ Wednesday 14 October 2026 -11:55PM ; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:   PD_JR-017516 Research Strategy Project Officer.docx  PD_JR-017516 Research Strategy Project Officer.pdf"
