    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Thomas-and-Margaret-Ruth-McArthur-Research-Fellow_JR-018964": {
      "title": "Thomas and Margaret Ruth McArthur Research Fellow",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-06\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full-time; Fixed-term (to 31 December 2029) Faculty: Arts Department/School:  School of Social and Political Sciences Salary: Level A $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super  Prestigious social sciences postdoctoral fellowship / Dedicated research funding up to $10,000 p.a. / Mentoring in a world-class interdisciplinary School Apply now to deepen your research profile and shape debates in anthropology and the social sciences. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   In this prestigious fellowship, you will drive independent research in anthropology or cognate social sciences disciplines. You will strengthen your publication profile, pursue external funding, and collaborate within a vibrant interdisciplinary School. What you will deliver:   Lead high-quality independent research in anthropology or cognate social sciences, aligned with School and Faculty priorities. Produce scholarly publications and conference presentations that enhance the School's research reputation. Develop and submit competitive external research grant applications, supported by dedicated fellowship research funds. Contribute to the School's academic life through limited supervision, guest lecturing, and collegial service activities. Maintain safe work practices and comply with OH&S and EH&S responsibilities relevant to your research. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD in Anthropology or a cognate social sciences discipline. Demonstrate capacity for independent scholarship and impactful research in the nominated field. Bring a publication record in refereed journals or publishing houses, relative to opportunity. Communicate clearly and confidently, with strong written and verbal skills for diverse audiences. Collaborate effectively and contribute collegially to an inclusive Faculty of Arts community. Align with Faculty values of diversity, inclusion, and high professional standards. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – School of Social and Political Sciences Within the Faculty of Arts, the School of Social and Political Sciences is a leader in social research. The School brings together Anthropology, Development Studies, Criminology, Gender Studies, Political Science, and Sociology. You will join a collegial community committed to impactful scholarship, public engagement, and distinctive social science education. Senior academics will support your development through mentoring, collaboration, and opportunities to build an enduring research profile. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Associate Professor Jeff Garmany at jeff.garmany@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 1 November 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description: Word: The Thomas and Margaret Ruth McArthur Research Fellow.docx PDF: The Thomas and Margaret Ruth McArthur Research Fellow.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/International-Partnerships-Adviser_JR-019055": {
      "title": "International Partnerships Adviser",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-05\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nole type: Full-time; Fixed term until 31 January 2028  Department Chancellery – Global, Culture and Engagement  Salary: UOM 7: $111,275 - $120,453 p.a. plus 17% super   Strategic global partnerships portfolio / Focus on Northeast Asia / Work closely with senior University leaders  Apply now to lead strategic international initiatives that strengthen the University’s partnerships, influence and global reach.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity:   You will support strategic international partnerships, providing analysis, advice and coordination that advance the University’s global ambitions.  You will develop relationships across Northeast Asia and beyond, identifying education and research collaboration opportunities and innovative partnership models.  What you will deliver:   Produce intelligence briefs, market analyses and reports to inform international engagement and partnership decisions in Northeast Asia and other regions.  Contribute to business cases, proposals and implementation plans for new institutional partnerships and strategic international projects.  Support development and delivery of regional strategies, education and research collaborations, and innovative transnational education initiatives.  Coordinate international visits, delegations and events, ensuring effective programming, stakeholder engagement and timely follow-up actions.  Maintain high-quality partnership management processes, including relationship tracking, governance support and continuous improvement of systems and frameworks.  You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a relevant degree, or bring an equivalent combination of education, training and international engagement experience.  Demonstrate strong written communication skills, including preparing briefs, reports, correspondence and presentations for senior stakeholders.  Manage multiple projects and administrative activities concurrently, exercising sound judgement, diplomacy, discretion and careful attention to detail.  Build effective relationships with international partners, understanding cross-cultural sensitivities and engaging confidently across diverse contexts.  Apply experience in process development and improvement to enhance partnership management, reporting and evaluation practices.  Bring experience in higher education, work in another country, or fluency in an Asian language.  What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave.  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team:   Chancellery Global, Culture and Engagement leads the University’s international strategy, partnerships and global profile.  Guided by the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Global, the team shapes regional strategies and advances Advancing Melbourne Globally 2030.  You will join talented colleagues who collaborate across faculties and Chancellery, engaging with governments, partners and senior leaders.  We offer a dynamic, outward-facing environment where your insights help deliver meaningful international impact for the University.  Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter addressing Essential Selection Criteria 1, 2, 3 found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria   Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Julie Fam at j.fam@unimelb.edu.au    For questions related to our recruitment process email Alessandro Storani at alessandro.storani@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: ​ Sunday 18 October 2026 - 11:55 PM ; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:    PD_JR-019055 International Partnerships Adviser .pdf PD_JR-019055 International Partnerships Adviser .pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/AURIN-Associate-Software-Engineer_JR-018050": {
      "title": "AURIN Associate Software Engineer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-05\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-term Faculty: Architecture, Building and Planning Department/School: Australian Urban Research Infrastructure Network (AURIN) Salary: AUD $100,847 - $109,163 p.a. plus 17% super  Entry-level software role in national research infrastructure / build modern cloud, data and API skills / support better urban decisions undefined Join AURIN to grow your engineering career while building platforms that support impactful city and regional research. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   As Associate Software Engineer, you will build and enhance AURIN’s cloud-first platforms for urban research. You will develop technical skills across modern languages, data systems and cloud tools while collaborating with experts. What you will deliver:   Contribute to designing, developing, testing and maintaining software components that power AURIN’s digital research infrastructure platforms. Implement integrations and RESTful services that connect data, analytical tools and visualisation systems for researchers and policymakers. Work within a team-based squad, pairing with senior engineers to solve technical challenges and deliver sprint objectives. Apply best-practice version control, CI/CD and containerisation workflows to support reliable, scalable deployments. Participate in documentation, support, and continuous improvement activities, ensuring systems remain usable, secure and well-governed. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold entry-level experience across the software development lifecycle, from requirements gathering through implementation, testing and maintenance. Possess foundational skills in languages such as Java, Python and JavaScript for application development. Understand web servers and databases, including technologies like Apache or Nginx, MongoDB and PostgreSQL. Work effectively within small collaborative teams and balance competing priorities to meet agreed deadlines. Use modern collaborative engineering practices, including git-based workflows, CI/CD, agile delivery and containerised deployments. Demonstrate motivation to up-skill through mentoring, self-directed learning and formal professional development opportunities. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team AURIN sits within the Faculty of Architecture, Building and Planning, delivering nationally significant urban research infrastructure. You will join a team of engineers, data specialists and researchers enabling evidence-based decisions for cities and regions. The team values collaboration, learning and innovation, providing mentoring, modern tools and opportunities to grow your technical capabilities. By contributing to AURIN’s platforms, you will support research on sustainability, housing, transport and urban resilience across Australia. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. This role is eligible for visa sponsorship and we welcome international applicants. If successful, we will support you through this process. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For questions related to our recruitment process email Brett Verity at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Position Description:   JR-018050 AURIN Associate Software Engineer PD.docx JR-018050 AURIN Associate Software Engineer PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Financial-Aid-Advisor_JR-018524": {
      "title": "Financial Aid Advisor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-05\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed term for 12 months Department: Student and Scholarly Services Salary: UOM 5 - $86,943 - $99,864 p.a. plus 17% super Meaningful impact on students’ financial wellbeing / shape evolving financial aid and arrival services / thrive in a supportive team Apply now to help students navigate financial assistance options, manage key arrival services, and enhance a vital support offering. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity In this role, you will support students seeking financial assistance and coordinate arrival services for new students.    You will process interest-free loans and hardship grants, manage student enquiries, and connect students with wider wellbeing support.    You will also contribute to service redesign and continuous improvement in a collaborative and supportive team. What you will deliver: Assess student loan and hardship grant applications, ensuring accurate eligibility decisions and timely processing of payments. Engage directly with students via appointments and enquiries, triaging cases, escalating appropriately, and referring to specialist services. Oversee day-to-day delivery of arrival services, liaising with external contractors and stakeholders to ensure smooth operations. Maintain accurate service information and templates across channels, including Teams, OneCRM and student-facing web content. Contribute to service improvement initiatives, supporting digital uplifts, process redesigns, and reviews that enhance student experience.   You may be a great fit if you: Hold a tertiary qualification in a relevant discipline, or equivalent education and experience in student services. Demonstrate experience in university student services or lending environments, working with financial hardship and wellbeing contexts. Manage time-critical workloads and competing demands with strong organisational skills and attention to detail. Operate confidently within protocols, policies and compliance requirements, applying privacy and confidentiality in record-keeping. Use CRM systems such as Salesforce, plus Microsoft Teams, Word, Excel and Outlook in daily work. Contribute proactively to a small, collaborative team, adapting to service redesign and continuous improvement activities.   For further information please refer to the attached PD. What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team – Student and Scholarly Services Student and Scholarly Services sits within Chancellery and provides end-to-end administration, wellbeing and scholarly support. Within this area, the Financial Aid team supports students through loans, hardship grants and coordinated arrival services. You will join a lean, positive team that values collaboration, flexibility, innovation and enhancing the student experience. Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at oied-hr@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Want to know more? For questions related to our recruitment process email Alex Spezza at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday the 18th of October 2026 at 11:55 PM; AEDT. Position Description: JR-018524 - Financial Aid Advisor - PD.pdf JR-018524 - Financial Aid Advisor - PD.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Lecturer--Nursing_JR-018635": {
      "title": "Lecturer, Nursing",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-05\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nRole type:  0.5-1FTE; Fixed term for 12 Months Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department/School: Department of Nursing; Melbourne School of Health Sciences Salary: $124,656 - $148,023 (pro-rata for part time) plus 17% super  Lead entry-to-practice and postgraduate mental health nursing education / shape evidence-based curriculum and simulation / partner with leading clinical services Contribute to the Department’s commitment to fostering excellent mental health nursing education that advances practice, supports students and deepens impactful clinical partnerships. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will coordinate and teach mental health nursing across postgraduate programs, shaping high-quality, evidence-based learning experiences. You will connect classroom, simulation and clinical practice, working closely with students and expert clinical partners. You will also contribute to scholarship in learning and teaching within a supportive, research-informed academic community. What you will deliver:   Coordinate and teach mental health nursing subjects across entry-to-practice and postgraduate programs using contemporary blended and online approaches. Develop, review and enhance curriculum and simulation activities grounded in evidence-based mental health nursing practice. Translate research and scholarship into innovative teaching that improves student learning and professional readiness. Build and maintain strong relationships with clinical partners and contribute expertise to professional and community activities. Engage actively in departmental and school committees, supporting inter-professional learning and strategic educational initiatives.   You may be a great fit if you:   Hold current AHPRA registration as a Registered Nurse and a master’s degree in nursing. Possess postgraduate qualifications and recent clinical experience in mental health nursing practice. Demonstrate advanced therapeutic skills, including expertise in child safety standards and family violence responses. Bring experience delivering mental health nursing education and coordinating programs or subjects within nursing. Maintain an established scholarship profile in learning and teaching, including publications or conference presentations. Communicate and collaborate effectively with students, colleagues and external stakeholders to support high-quality education. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team You will join the Department of Nursing within the Melbourne School of Health Sciences at the Parkville campus. The team is committed to excellence in nursing education, research and clinical partnership, with strong connections to leading health services. Colleagues value collaboration, compassion, respect, integrity and accountability, and champion diversity and inclusion. You will work alongside experienced educators and researchers who are dedicated to improving mental health care and enhancing student success. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and relevant experience and addressing the Key Selection Criteria (KSC) outlined in the Position Description in one document . Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Fiona Newell at fnewall@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Tara at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Please note - Applications will be reviewed as they are received. Applications close: Monday 19th October 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-018635 Lecturer, Nursing - PD.docx JR-018635 Lecturer, Nursing - PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Curator--Rare-Books_JR-018643-1": {
      "title": "Curator, Rare Books",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-12\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nPosition Number: JR-018643 Role type: Full-time; Continuing Location: Parkville Department: Archives and Special Collections (Student and Scholarly Services) Salary: UOM 7: AUD $111,275 - $120,453 p.a. plus 17% super Steward an internationally significant rare books collection and join the team leading  creative public engagement initiatives Apply now to curate a collection that powers research, teaching innovation and inspires public engagement at Melbourne. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity In this role, you will curate and interpret the University’s Rare Books Collection for diverse audiences. You will help drive object-based learning, research and exhibitions, while enhancing access through contributing to cataloguing, digitisation and outreach. You will shape how this extraordinary collection supports innovative scholarship across disciplines. What you will deliver: Provide oversight for physical access, storage, handling, movement and loans, ensuring best practice care of rare materials. Develop exhibitions and interpretive programs, underpinned by collection-based research, to broaden discovery and engagement. Design and deliver object-based learning and collection literacy programs embedded within curriculum and e-learning environments. Contribute to digitisation, catalogue enrichment and rights management to improve discovery and responsible access to materials. Advise on acquisitions, donations and deaccessions, ensuring compliant trust fund use and robust collection documentation.   You may be a great fit if you: Hold postgraduate qualifications in Museum or Curatorial Studies, or equivalent experience with historical or cultural collections. Possess tertiary qualifications in humanities or Library Science, or an equivalent blend of education and experience. Bring deep knowledge of rare and antiquarian books, manuscripts and print history, including collecting and publishing contexts. Demonstrate experience curating exhibitions and access programs, and interpreting complex material for diverse and non-specialist audiences. Apply advanced skills in cataloguing, condition reporting, collection management systems and safe handling of cultural heritage materials. Collaborate confidently with academics, donors, booksellers and professional staff within large, multifaceted institutions or libraries. Embrace emerging digital, metadata and learning technologies to enhance collection discovery, teaching integration and online engagement. For further information please refer to the attached PD. What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team   You will join Archives and Special Collections, a specialist department within Student and Scholarly Services. The team develops, cares for and shares outstanding archives and special collections for teaching, learning and research. You will work with collegial experts who value collaboration, innovation, service excellence and inclusive, community-focused engagement. Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff. Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Jon Buckingham via jon.buckingham@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Tien Nguyen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Monday 26th October at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone. Position Description: PD_JR-018643 Curator, Rare Books.docx PD_JR-018643 Curator, Rare Books.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/STEM-Outreach-Programs-Coordinator--STEM-EDGE_JR-018864": {
      "title": "STEM Outreach Programs Coordinator, STEM EDGE",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-12\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-Term for 12 months   Faculty: Faculty of Science  Department/ School: Student and Academic Services   Salary: UoM 6: $100,847 - $109,163 p.a. plus 17% super  Champion equity-focused STEM outreach for girls and gender diverse students / coordinate flagship programs across Science / grow your impact in a supportive, inclusive faculty.  Apply now to share your STEM passion, equity values and personal story while shaping students' futures.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.     The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity   In this identified role, you will coordinate STEM EDGE and contribute to programs that inspire young people to explore opportunities in STEM.  You will coordinate engaging camps, events and mentoring experiences that build confidence, develop skills and connect students with STEM role models.  You’ll be part of a values-driven team committed to equity and improving the representation of women and gender-diverse people in science.  As part of the Faculty’s commitment to improving representation in science, this role is open only to applicants who are women or gender diverse, in line with the special measure provided for under section 12 of the Equal Opportunity Act 2010 (Vic).   What you will deliver:    Plan, develop and coordinate STEM EDGE, The Science Experience and other outreach programs within the Faculty.  Promote programs, recruit participants and schools, and manage logistics to ensure safe, engaging and inclusive experiences.  Build and maintain relationships with students, families, schools, mentors and University staff to support participation and impact.  Recruit, train and support student mentors and volunteers, fostering community and positive role modelling for participants.  Monitor program outcomes, evaluate and report data, and identify risks and improvements, including child safety requirements  You may be a great fit if you:    Hold a relevant tertiary qualification or equivalent combination of education, training and related experience.  Demonstrate passion for STEM education and enthusiasm for engaging school students, especially girls and gender diverse students.  Bring project management skills to deliver programs on time, within budget and with evidenced positive outcomes.  Possess experience working with schools or youth programs and understanding of duty-of-care and child safety responsibilities.  Show awareness of equity issues and barriers facing girls and gender diverse students pursuing STEM pathways.  Demonstrate confidence communicating with varied stakeholders and initiative to host events, workshops and student activities.  For further information please refer to the attached PD.   What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program     For more information check out our benefits page!   Your new team – Student and Academic Services   You will join the Faculty of Science’s Student and Academic Services team, dedicated to transformative STEM education. The team leads flagship outreach programs that connect diverse school communities with inspiring scientists and authentic university experiences.   We champion equity, inclusion and belonging, particularly for girls and gender diverse students exploring STEM futures. You will collaborate across schools, institutes and partners, gaining broad networks and support to grow your outreach expertise.  Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.  A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria     Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Marie Kinsey at marie.kinsey@unimelb.edu.au    For questions related to our recruitment process email Oliver Bukasa at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').     Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: 25 October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:    JR-018864 - STEM Outreach Programs Coordinator, STEM EDGE - PD.docx   JR-018864 - STEM Outreach Programs Coordinator, STEM EDGE - PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Research-Software-Engineer_JR-018676": {
      "title": "Research Software Engineer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-09\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-term (2 years) Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department/School: Melbourne School of Population and Global Health, Centre for Epidemiology and Biostatistics Salary: UOM 6 - $100,847 - $109,163 p.a. plus 17% superannuation Shape epidemic forecasting infrastructure across Australia and New Zealand / focus on professional software engineering, not academic publishing / collaborate with leading epidemiologists, statisticians and data scientists Apply now to use your software engineering expertise to deliver robust epidemic analytics tools that inform policy Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities. The University of Melbourne We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity You will design and deliver open-source software that powers national epidemic forecasting and scenario modelling hubs. You will work closely with leading epidemiologists and data scientists, turning complex health data into actionable insights. This professional software engineering role lets you focus on building high-quality tools rather than academic publications. What you will deliver: Plan, design, develop, test and deploy open-source research software that supports ACEFA’s forecasting and scenario modelling hubs. Create modern web interfaces, dashboards and user experiences, integrating secure back-end services, databases and cloud infrastructure. Engage software users and collaborators through code repositories and forums, supporting multi-team participation in epidemic analytics. Implement robust data management, security and confidentiality practices aligned with population health research policies and standards. Produce clear technical documentation, reports and project communications that enable reuse, scalability and ongoing maintenance of tools.   You may be a great fit if you: Hold a bachelor’s degree in computer science, software engineering or a similarly computational discipline. Design and implement high-quality research software using R, Python, JavaScript or comparable data-focused programming languages. Develop modern web interfaces and visualisations using JavaScript frameworks, HTML5, CSS3 and relevant libraries. Apply strong software engineering practices, including Git, testing, continuous integration and high-performance computing skills. Manage project workload effectively, meeting milestones and deadlines while maintaining clean, reproducible and well-documented code. Collaborate within multidisciplinary teams, communicating complex technical concepts clearly to non-technical public health stakeholders.   For further information please refer to the attached PD. What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program For more information check out our benefits page!   Your new team – Infectious Disease Dynamics Unit You will join the Infectious Disease Dynamics Unit within the Centre for Epidemiology and Biostatistics. Our team collaborates across universities, government and ARDC partners to tackle pressing infectious disease threats. You will work in a values-driven, multidisciplinary environment that blends data science, public health and impactful research. Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application. Want to know more? For questions related to our recruitment process email Alex Spezza at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 18th of October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone. Position Description: JR-018676 - Research Software Engineer - PD.docx JR-018676 - Research Software Engineer - PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Shepparton-Victoria/Deputy-Director--Medical-Student-Education--Echuca_JR-018936-1": {
      "title": "Deputy Director, Medical Student Education- Echuca",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-09\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nRole type:  2x 0.25 FTE positions; Continuing Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department:  Department of Rural Health Salary: Level C: $152,695 - $176,065 per annum (pro rata for part-time) p.a. plus 17% super  Shape rural medical education for MD students / build partnerships with regional health services / lead a supportive student culture Join us to lead clinical teaching in regional Victoria and support the next generation of rural doctors. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead and deliver high quality clinical education for MD students based at our Echuca campus. You will shape rural medical training, support student wellbeing, and work with clinicians across the Rural Clinical School. What you will deliver:   Lead implementation and continual improvement of the clinical curriculum for MD students within the Rural Clinical School. Collaborate with Echuca Regional Health and Deputy Directors at other sites to coordinate teaching and student rotations. Provide regular academic and pastoral support, including weekly meetings, to ensure students thrive in regional placements. Deliver high quality clinical teaching, assessment, and course coordination aligned with University policies and standards. Engage in research and scholarly activity, including supervising MD research students and contributing to rural health projects.   You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a medical qualification recognised in Australia, plus college fellowship or equivalent clinically relevant postgraduate degree. Bring extensive experience in clinical teaching, medical education, and curriculum delivery for medical students. Maintain active clinical practice in the Wangaratta region and demonstrate national recognition within your clinical specialty. Demonstrate strong course coordination, organisational, and administrative skills within a university or teaching hospital environment. Build collaborative relationships with students, clinicians, and health services, providing high quality academic and pastoral support. Show familiarity with Victorian regional hospitals and an interest in rural health and rural medical education. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – The Department of Rural Health sits within the Faculty of Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences and delivers rural clinical education across multiple regional sites, including Echuca, Ballarat, Shepparton and Wangaratta. The team works closely with partner health services to provide high quality medical training that strengthens rural workforces and improves community health outcomes. You will join a supportive, values-driven culture that champions collaboration, compassion, respect, integrity and accountability, and invests in staff development and wellbeing. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Steven Bismire For questions related to our recruitment process email Walid at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 23 October. 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-018936 Deputy Director, Medical Student Education- Echuca PD.docx JR-018936 Deputy Director, Medical Student Education- Echuca PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Lawyer--MDHS-_JR-018932-1": {
      "title": "Lawyer (MDHS)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-09\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Continuing  Department: Legal and Risk, COO Portfolio  Salary: UOM 8 - $125,191 - $135,504 p.a. plus 17% super  Advise on complex health, research and commercial law matters / partner with Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences (MDHS) and Melbourne Research and Enterprise (MRE) leaders / grow versatile in-house legal expertise  Apply now to shape strategic legal outcomes for health, research and education across a leading university.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity:   You will provide high-quality legal advice on complex health, research and commercial matters across MDHS and MRE. You will work as a trusted partner to senior stakeholders, shaping strategic decisions and managing legal risk.  What you will deliver:   Provide clear, solution-focused legal advice on areas including research, clinical trials, IP, employment, regulation and student placements.  Draft, review and negotiate diverse commercial agreements, including research, technology, licensing and collaboration arrangements.  Build strong partnerships with MDHS and MRE stakeholders, enabling early legal input into key initiatives.  Support Legal and Risk projects, driving process improvements and consistent, efficient legal service delivery.  Manage routine and complex matters independently while collaborating closely with colleagues across the MDHS cluster and the Legal and Risk team.  You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a law degree, at least two years’ PQE and a current Victorian Practising Certificate.  Bring experience in commercial contracts and one or more of research, clinical trials, IP or digital health.  Demonstrate strong drafting, analytical and negotiation skills, with a focus on practical, solution-oriented advice.  Manage competing priorities and ambiguity while exercising sound judgment about when to escalate or seek guidance.  Build productive relationships with diverse stakeholders and contribute positively to a collaborative, high-performing team.  Maintain high ethical standards, professionalism and confidentiality when dealing with sensitive or complex legal matters.  What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as  learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team:   Legal Services is the University’s in-house legal team providing strategic legal support across teaching, research and operations. You will join the MDHS cluster, working closely with leaders in medicine, dentistry, health sciences and research. The team champions Service Excellence, One Team and University First, fostering collaboration, learning and continuous improvement.  Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please use the cover letter and resume template (under Application Document) below to create and outline your experience. Resumes not in the template below will not be accepted.    Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Alex Maschmedt at  amaschmedt@unimelb.edu.au    For questions related to our recruitment process email Ethan Khamly at ethan.khamly@unimelb.edu.au    For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications Close: ​ Sunday 25th October 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.   Application Document: Template CV - UOM8 Lawyer (MDHS)- 2026.docx   Position Description:   JR-018932 Lawyer (MDHS) PD.pdf   JR-018932 Lawyer (MDHS) PD.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Senior-Lecturer-Social-Change-and-Education-Policy_JR-019132": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer Social Change and Education Policy",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-08\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full time, fixed term (four years) Faculty: Education Department/School:  Education and Society Salary: Level C - $152,695 - $176,065 p.a. plus 17% super  Shape education systems through critical policy scholarship / drive social change with impactful research / teach diverse future educators Join a collaborative academic community to advance education policy scholarship and inspire students committed to social justice Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead innovative teaching and research on social change, education policy and governance. You will design and coordinate subjects across undergraduate and postgraduate programs, including online and blended delivery. You will collaborate with colleagues and partners to influence educational futures locally, nationally and internationally. What you will deliver:   Design, deliver and coordinate engaging subjects on social change and education policy across undergraduate and postgraduate programs. Embed Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander education and diverse social justice perspectives throughout curricula and teaching practice. Build a high-quality research programme, securing competitive funding and publishing in leading international outlets. Supervise Graduate Research students and contribute to research projects focused on education policy and social change. Contribute academic leadership and service through committees, professional associations and collaborative partnerships with external stakeholders. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD or research doctorate in education or a closely related discipline. Demonstrate strong expertise in education policy, governance and social change, including contemporary theoretical and policy debates. Show a developing track record of high-quality publications and success in securing competitive research funding. Bring experience designing, coordinating and delivering tertiary subjects, with confidence using digital and blended pedagogies. Provide evidence of effective Graduate Research supervision or co-supervision within relevant fields. Engage collaboratively across disciplines and with external partners, including communities, governments and professional associations. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – Faculty of Education Within the Faculty of Education, the School of Education and Society brings together scholars committed to understanding education as a public, relational and ethical endeavour. The team leads impactful research and teaching that informs policy, professional practice and system reform, with a strong focus on equity, inclusion and sustainability. You will join a collegial community that values innovation, partnership and research-led teaching to shape more just educational futures. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Marcia McKenzie at marcia.mckenzie@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 25 October 2026 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description - Word: JR-019132_Senior Lecturer, Social Change and Education Policy_PD.docx PDF: JR-019132_Senior Lecturer, Social Change and Education Policy_PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Lecturer-Health-and-Physical-Education_JR-019063": {
      "title": "Lecturer Health and Physical Education",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-08\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full time; Fixed term to 30 November 2027 Faculty: Education Department/School:  School of Education and Society Salary: Level B - $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super  Advance excellence in teaching, research, and leadership within the Health, Outdoor, Physical Education and Sport disciplines, contributing meaningfully to both the Faculty of Education and broader academic community.  Engage with innovative pedagogies and interdisciplinary scholarship, and shape the future of education in a dynamic and supportive environment.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will be a member of an academic team within the Health, Outdoor, Physical Education and Sport (HOPES) specialisation in the Faculty of Education, and play a pivotal role in advancing teaching, scholarship, engagement, and leadership while also contributing more broadly to the fields of teaching and teacher education. You will coordinate and deliver subjects at both undergraduate and graduate levels, with a particular emphasis on the Faculty of Education’s undergraduate breadth subjects and graduate initial teacher education programmes. What you will deliver:   Preparing and delivering diverse teaching modalities, developing and reviewing curricula, and coordinating subject administration including supervision of sessional staff.  Assessing student progress through timely feedback, marking, moderation, supporting practical application of theory, and monitoring teaching quality for continuous improvement.  Conducting and disseminating scholarship in the Health, Outdoor, Physical Education and Sport (HOPES) field, securing research funding, supervising graduate research students, and engaging in national and international academic forums.  Providing leadership through contributing to professional development, participating in faculty and university committees, supporting colleagues, and engaging with relevant professional and community organisations.    You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a PhD or research doctorate relevant to the Health, Outdoor, Physical Education and Sport (HOPES) specialist area with a focus on education and teaching.  Demonstrate experience in course or subject development, subject coordination, and providing specialised support to students on placement.  Maintain a developing research profile with a strong record of publications in leading academic outlets and the ability to contribute to research funding and supervision of graduate research students.  Possess proficiency in digital literacy and familiarity with contemporary digital pedagogies to support innovative research, learning, and teaching practices.  For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team – Faculty of Education The Faculty of Education (FoE) fosters staff productivity, growth and engagement in a collective effort to enrich the contribution that education makes to society. We conduct research and teaching that leads to the transformation of education practice both within and beyond the profession. Faculty of Education stimulates learning that enriches the potential of students from around the world, enabling meaningful careers and profound contributions to communities. We provide research leadership, setting the direction for high-impact, innovative and responsive research that addresses the pressing issues of our time. We lead purposeful engagement with society, sharing our resources and expertise as part of collaborative efforts to build a resilient, equitable and sustainable future.  Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact John Quay at jquay@unimelb.edu.au . For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Petersen at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description: JR-019063_Lecturer, Health and Physical Education_PD.docx"
